# Dr Brain - Delivery — LLM Guide

Dr Brain - Delivery is a manual fleet game for humans and AI agents. You run one
city: assign orders to cars, drive each car to its kitchen (pickup is automatic),
then to its customer (dropoff is automatic), and deliver before the deadline.
There is no win condition; the city grows on a fixed tick schedule, demand rises,
and the fleet is limited only by money.

Humans: open {{.BaseURL}}/ , pick a city, and drive with `w a s d`, the arrow keys
or the on-screen D-pad. The game starts paused so orders cannot expire while you
read the screen.

GraphQL endpoint:       POST {{.GraphQLURL}}   (one schema, namespace: delivery)
GraphQL subscriptions:  {{.GraphQLWSURL}}  (graphql-ws protocol, root field `deliverySession`)
Playground:             GET  {{.PlaygroundURL}}
Introspection:          enabled unless the operator disabled it — query __schema/__type

Every example below is valid against the live schema. Replace SESSION_ID with an
id returned by `createSession`, CAR_ID / ORDER_ID with ids from a snapshot, and
OWNER_TOKEN with the secret `createSession` returned.

There is no MCP endpoint for Delivery. Use GraphQL.

---

## Ownership

- A session id is public: anyone with it may read `session`, `sessions`, the
  `carView`/`events` queries and the `deliverySession` subscription, and may watch
  it live.
- Only the session's creator may change it. `createSession` returns `ownerToken`,
  a secret shown once. Pass it as `ownerToken` on every mutation. A signed-in
  creator may instead use the account they created the session with.
- `canPlaySession(id, ownerToken)` answers whether this caller may change the
  session; it returns `false` for an unknown id rather than an error.
- A missing or wrong credential is FORBIDDEN; an unknown id is NOT_FOUND. Sessions
  created before owner tokens existed stay open to anyone.
- Sessions start paused, including restored saves. A save remembers the speed it
  was left at but reloads paused; the client must opt into the clock again.

## Commands vs. the clock

- `moveCar` appends one step to that car's movement queue. It does **not** move
  the car and does not advance the clock. A car runs at most one queued step per
  tick, in order, so you can preload a route (up to 64 steps per car) and then
  advance the clock. The overflow is `LIMIT_REACHED`.
- `clearMoves` drops a car's whole queue, including the step for the next tick.
  Use it when traffic, closures or a better order invalidate a preloaded route.
- `tick` applies one queued step per car, advances the world by `count` ticks
  (default 1) and returns the new snapshot. A rejected step (wall, battery,
  closure, occupied tile) is spent, not retried; the rest of the queue stays.
- `setSpeed` changes the clock multiplier: 0 pauses, 1/2/4 run in real time.
  Use `setSpeed(id, speed: 0, ...)` and `tick` for deterministic agent play.
- `waitUntil` blocks until the running clock reaches a target and returns that
  snapshot. Pass exactly one of `tick` (an absolute future tick) or `ticks` (a
  count from the tick when the call starts, at most 1000). A paused session
  cannot advance, so the call fails with `INVALID_ARGUMENT` instead of hanging —
  including when the session is paused while the call is already waiting. Use
  `tick` when you want progress without a running clock.
- Sessions are serialized on the server; every mutation returns the full snapshot,
  so there is no follow-up read needed after one succeeds.

## Coordinates and the map

- The grid is `gridSize x gridSize`. `x` grows to the right; `y` grows downward.
  Row 0 is the TOP row. NORTH = y-1, SOUTH = y+1, EAST = x+1, WEST = x-1.
- `session.city.rows` is the current map as glyph rows. `hiddenTiles` is the
  number of unexplored tiles.
- Glyphs: `.` road, `,` ground (passable, costs one extra battery), `#` water
  (blocked), `B` building (blocked), `D` depot (free charging and parking),
  `C` charger (paid charging), `K` kitchen, `H` customer waiting, `h` customer
  house with no live order, `?` unexplored (blocked), `X` closed for street works
  (blocked), digits are cars. `rows` collapses cars on the same tile to one digit;
  the `cars` list is the authority.

## Rules

- A car spends `carClasses[].stepCost` battery per step; ground adds the city's
  ground surcharge. Moving into water, a building, an unexplored tile, a closed
  tile, off the map, or a road tile occupied by another car is rejected: position
  and battery are unchanged, and `lastReject` records why on the next tick.
- Cars may always leave a tile that closes under them. Following is allowed: a
  car may enter a tile a car ahead vacated on the same tick. A head-on pair cannot
  pass through each other.
- A depot accepts any number of cars and charges for free; a standalone charger
  holds its `capacity` and bills per tick. A car with no battery and no route to a
  charger is `STRANDED`; `towCar` returns it to the nearest depot for a fee.
- Pickup and dropoff are automatic. `ASSIGNED` means drive to the kitchen;
  `PICKED_UP` means drive to the customer; then the order becomes `DONE`.
- An order's `quantity` is boxes. `assignOrder` refuses an order that does not fit
  beside the boxes the car already carries AND the boxes promised to its other
  assigned-but-not-yet-picked-up orders. A car may hold several orders at once,
  but their total must fit; the error names the carried, assigned and capacity
  figures. `freeBoxes` is only the currently empty hold, so subtract outstanding
  assignments yourself before batching. Buy a van or truck at a depot when the
  work outgrows the scooter; the depot id comes from `session.city.depots`.
- `ticksLeft` is signed: a negative value means the deadline has passed but the
  tick has not swept the order yet. Assigning refreshes the deadline from the
  courier's current position. Late deliveries pay less; expired orders cost money
  and reputation (up to -0.10, scaled by standing: full at 1.0+, a fifth at 0.2).
  Reputation is clamped to 0..5 and a new session starts at the top of the scale
  (5.00), so it pays in full until orders are missed.
- Reputation sets what customers pay: `ledger.payMultiplier` is 1.0 at 4.90+,
  0.9 at 4.50, 0.8 at 4.00, 0.7 at 3.50, 0.6 at 3.00, 0.5 at 2.50,
  0.4 at 2.00, 0.3 at 1.50, 0.2 at 1.00, 0.1 at 0.50, and a 0.1 floor for any
  standing above zero (0 only at exactly 0.00). An
  on-time delivery pays `round(reward x payMultiplier)` and still earns +0.02
  reputation even when the multiplier is 0, so free runs rebuild standing.
- Fleet upkeep charges cars not parked on a depot every
  `fleet.upkeep_interval_ticks`. `ledger.upkeepDue` shows the next charge.
  `ledger.upkeepPaid` and `ledger.towFees` are cumulative costs.
- Growth stages open districts, kitchens, customers, chargers and depot bays
  automatically. Hidden tiles become passable only when a stage reveals them.
  Count the applied stages from `growth { done }`; there is no separate chapter
  field in the API.
- Street works (`closures`, `X`) are temporary and derived from the session seed
  and tick. Closed tiles cannot be entered; a car already on one may drive off.

### Order states

| state | meaning |
|---|---|
| `PENDING` | no car assigned |
| `ASSIGNED` | assigned; drive to the kitchen for pickup |
| `PICKED_UP` | carrying; drive to the customer for dropoff |
| `DONE` | delivered; `paid` and `late` are meaningful |
| `EXPIRED` | never delivered; a fine and reputation hit |

`assignedCar` stays set after a terminal state: read `state` to decide what is in
flight, and use `assignedCar` as history once the order is `DONE`/`EXPIRED`.

## Quick start

### 1. List cities / create a session

```graphql
query {
  delivery {
    cities { id name gridSize status ownedByMe builtin ownerless }
  }
}
```

```graphql
mutation {
  delivery {
    createSession(cityId: "city") {
      session { id tick paused speed tickMs }
      ownerToken
    }
  }
}
```

`createSession` takes optional `id` (generated when omitted), `seed`
(reproducible city) and `cityId` (omitted = the server's default). It snapshots
the city, so later city edits never change a running game. Keep `ownerToken`.

### 2. Read the session

```graphql
query {
  delivery {
    session(id: "SESSION_ID") {
      id displayName tick paused speed tickMs eventCapacity
      city {
        id name gridSize rows hiddenTiles
        kitchens { id name x y }
        customers { id name x y }
        depots { id name x y }
        chargers { id name x y }
      }
      cars {
        id name className x y battery maxBattery batteryPct capacity load freeBoxes
        cargo state queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject chargeDue
      }
      openOrders {
        id item itemName quantity kitchenId kitchenName customerId customerName
        state assignedCar createdTick deadlineTick ticksLeft reward paid late
      }
      ledger {
        money moneyEarned reputation payMultiplier deliveries lateDeliveries expiredOrders
        ordersSpawned onTimeStreak tows upkeepPaid towFees upkeepDue
        ticksPerMonth
        cashEntries { seq tick kind category amount balance carId carName orderId memo }
        months { index startTick endTick income expenses net categories { category income expenses } }
        deliveriesByItem { item count }
      }
      carClasses { className price capacity maxBattery stepCost unlocked affordable }
      growth { id tick title description done }
      events { tick type message carId x y }
      closures { id reason startTick endTick ticksLeft minX minY maxX maxY tiles { x y } }
    }
  }
}
```

`Query.city` is a static preview with every district revealed; `session.city`
is the live view and is the one to read while playing.

`openOrders` is the actionable list. `orders` takes filters and defaults to the
newest 2000 rows: `orders(state: [EXPIRED], sinceTick: 4000, limit: 50)`; pass
`includeFinished: false` for the open set.

`Query.events(id, sinceTick, limit)` returns a feed rather than a bare list:
`oldestTick`, `truncated` and `capacity` say whether the request reached past
the retained ring, so a poller can tell a quiet stretch from a gap it missed.
`Query.sessions` takes `sort` (CREATED_AT/TICK/MONEY), `limit` (≤500), `before`
and `mine`. `renameSession(id, displayName, ownerToken)` names a save.

`ledger.months` and `ledger.cashEntries` default to the newest three accounting
months. Both take filters, and the full history stays readable this way:

```graphql
query {
  delivery {
    session(id: "SESSION_ID") {
      ledger {
        months(month: 2) { index startTick endTick income expenses net
          categories { category income expenses } }
        cashEntries(month: 2, category: TOWS, carId: "car_1", limit: 50) {
          seq tick kind category amount balance memo
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

`month` is 1-based; `fromTick`/`toTick` are inclusive and intersect `month` when
both are given; `category` filters the bucket and makes a month row report only
that bucket; `carId` applies to statement rows only; `limit` caps rows, newest
first.

### 3. Assign an order

```graphql
mutation {
  delivery {
    assignOrder(id: "SESSION_ID", orderId: "ORDER_ID", carId: "CAR_ID", ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") {
      tick
      cars { id x y state queuedMove lastReject }
      orders { id state assignedCar ticksLeft }
    }
  }
}
```

Assign when the order fits beside the car's carried boxes and the boxes already
reserved by other orders assigned to that car; the error names the carried,
assigned and capacity figures otherwise. `unassignOrder(id, orderId, ownerToken)`
releases it and its reserved space.

### 4. Preload steps, then advance the clock

Each `moveCar` appends one step; repeat it for the route you want, then `tick`.
Queued steps run one per tick, in order. `cars[].queuedMoves` is the plan in
execution order (`queuedMove` is its head).

```graphql
mutation {
  delivery {
    moveCar(id: "SESSION_ID", carId: "CAR_ID", direction: NORTH, ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") {
      tick
      cars { id x y battery queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject }
    }
  }
}
```

```graphql
mutation {
  delivery {
    tick(id: "SESSION_ID", count: 3, ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") {
      tick
      cars { id x y battery state queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject }
      orders { id state ticksLeft }
      ledger { money deliveries lateDeliveries expiredOrders reputation payMultiplier }
    }
  }
}
```

Directions: `NORTH` `SOUTH` `EAST` `WEST`. Check the post-tick `lastReject` and
`cars[].x/y`; a rejected move costs nothing, so choose another route. If a
preloaded route is invalidated, `clearMoves(id, carId, ownerToken)` empties the
queue before you replan. Repeat until the order is `DONE`.

### 5. Other mutations

```graphql
mutation Pause { delivery { setSpeed(id: "SESSION_ID", speed: 0, ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { tick paused speed } } }
mutation Clear { delivery { clearMoves(id: "SESSION_ID", carId: "CAR_ID", ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { tick cars { id x y queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject } } } }
mutation Tow { delivery { towCar(id: "SESSION_ID", carId: "CAR_ID", ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { tick cars { id x y state battery } ledger { money towFees } } } }
mutation Buy { delivery { buyCar(id: "SESSION_ID", className: "van", depotId: "d_main", ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { tick cars { id name className capacity load freeBoxes state } ledger { money } } } }
mutation Save { delivery { saveSession(id: "SESSION_ID", ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { id tick } } }
mutation Wait { delivery { waitUntil(id: "SESSION_ID", ticks: 5, ownerToken: "OWNER_TOKEN") { tick paused cars { id x y state } orders { id state ticksLeft } } } }
```

`buyCar` needs a depot id from `session.city.depots`; `d_main` is the shipped
city's depot. `evictSession` saves and unloads; `deleteSession` permanently
removes the save.

### 6. Cheap polling and streaming

```graphql
query {
  delivery {
    carView(id: "SESSION_ID", carId: "CAR_ID", radius: 2) {
      rowSpan colSpan rows originX originY
      car { id name className x y battery maxBattery capacity load freeBoxes state queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject chargeDue }
      visibleCars { id name className x y battery maxBattery capacity load freeBoxes state queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject chargeDue }
      nearbyOrders { id itemName quantity kitchenId kitchenName customerId customerName state assignedCar ticksLeft reward }
    }
  }
}
```

```graphql
query {
  delivery {
    events(id: "SESSION_ID", sinceTick: 100) {
      oldestTick truncated capacity
      events { tick type message carId x y }
    }
  }
}
```

The subscription sends the same snapshot shape as `session` (finished orders are
trimmed to the most recent few):

```graphql
subscription {
  deliverySession(id: "SESSION_ID") {
    tick paused speed
    cars { id x y battery state queuedMove queuedMoves lastReject }
    orders { id state ticksLeft assignedCar }
    ledger { money deliveries expiredOrders reputation }
  }
}
```

## City authoring (optional)

Authenticated players can create drafts; publication runs the full startup
validation (grid, districts, orders, reachability, charging, growth).

```graphql
mutation {
  delivery {
    validateCity(config: "<raw city JSON>") { valid publishable issues }
    createCity(id: "my-city", config: "<raw city JSON>") { id name status }
    publishCity(id: "my-city") { id status }
  }
}
```

`cityConfig(id)` returns the decoded, defaulted authoring document. Builtin
cities and ownerless legacy cities need `X-Admin-Key` for writes; community
drafts need their owner. Sessions already started keep their city snapshot.

## Goal

Keep the fleet moving: assign each live order to a car that can carry it, drive
to the kitchen and then the customer, watch the deadline, and charge or tow when
needed. Report the final tick, money, reputation, deliveries, late deliveries and
expired orders. Do not delete the save or guess other sessions' ids.
